Dear Responsible Officer(s)/AML Compliance Officer,

Taishin International Bank Co., Ltd., HK Branch (“the Company”)

Further to our off-site review of the Company, which was conducted based on the Company’s internal policies, procedures, and relevant documentation relating to Anti-Money Laundering and Counter-Terrorist Financing (“AML/CTF”) provided to us, as well as the Company’s responses to our enquiries, we would like to draw your attention to the following observation(s) in respect of compliance with the GL3: –

Sanctions and PEP Screening

(i)	According to the Company’s existing control framework, identified third party payors are subject to sanctions screening before submission of the application documents to its principal (s), where such payors are identified.  However, it was not clearly stated in the Company’s AML Policy and Procedures that identified third party payors of life insurances are subject to sanctions screening.  

In this connection, for clarity enhancement, the Company should update the relevant internal policy and procedure wording to expressly state that identified third party payors are included among the connected parties subject to sanctions screening.

(ii)	Sections 3.11.5, 5.4.1, 5.5.1, 5.5.2, 5.5.3, 12.5.4 of the Company’s Anti-Money Laundering and Counter-Terrorist Financing Policy (version 8) ("AML Policy") stipulates that the Company's Customer Data Check ("客戶資料檢核") module of the Company's AML name checking system is used to perform batch screening on existing customer database to identify PEPs and sanctioned parties . However, it seems that this batch screening process only covers existing customers, beneficial owners, connected parties and PPTAs etc., who maintains a banking relationship with the Company.    It should be noted that even the customers closed their bank accounts with the Company, the Company is still acting as an insurance agency of those customers who still have inforce insurance policy (ies).  

In this connection, the Company should ensure that, even the customers closed the bank account, where an insurance policy remains in force, the relevant policyholders and beneficial owners remain subject to ongoing sanctions and PEP screening.  The ongoing sanctions screening should extend to connected parties and PPTAs of insurance customers using a risk- based approach for the Company’s insurance agency business.

Identification and Verification of Beneficiary 

(iii)	According to page 14 of the Company's AML Policy, an Insurance Institution (“II”), including a licensed insurance agency, should be required to (a) inform senior management before payout of the policy proceeds, (b) conduct enhanced scrutiny on the whole business relationship with the policyholder and (c) consider making a suspicious transaction report if the beneficiary or beneficial owner(s) of the beneficiary is determined as PEP where higher risks are identified.  However, it seems that the subject requirements were not incorporated in 《保險代理業務作業流程說明書》.  

In this connection, the Company should enhance relevant SOP wording to more explicitly reflect the relevant AML/CFT operational requirements.

Record Keeping

(iv)	Paragraph 8.3(d) of the GL3 requires an II, including a licensed insurance agency, to keep the original or copy of the records and documents relating to the customer’s account (e.g. account opening form, insurance application form or risk assessment form) and business correspondence with the customer and any beneficial owner of the customer (which at a minimum should include business correspondence material to CDD measures or significant changes to the operation of the account). Paragraph 8.4 of the GL3 further states that all documents and records mentioned in paragraph 8.3 should be kept throughout the continuance of the business relationship with the customer and for a period of at least five years after the end of the business relationship.

The Company’s 《保險代理業務作業流程說明書》currently provides a seven-year retention period for insurance applications and supporting documents following policy termination.  It appears that this practice fell short of the GL3 requirements, as retention must be maintained for at least five years after the termination of the business relationship, not merely after policy termination.  Consequently, under the Company’s current record-keeping procedures, where a policyholder (who is also a bank account holder of the Company) maintains multiple insurance policies, records relating to a policy that terminated seven years earlier than another may have already been disposed of.  Only records associated with the later-terminating policy, including application forms, claim forms, and servicing forms, would be kept by the Company, which constitutes a gap in compliance with the GL3.  

In this connection, the Company should enhance the relevant internal policy and procedural wording to make this retention arrangement for terminated policies under an ongoing insurance agency relationship more explicit.

Please note that the observations mentioned above are based on the documents and information supplied to us.  They may not cover all areas in your Company’s compliance with the GL3.  

Please review the aforesaid observation(s) and submit your Company’s action plan developed with the way and the timeline under which your Company intends to address the observation(s) for our review by 5 August 2026 (Wed).

Should you have any queries concerning the above, please contact me at 3899 9752 or James Luo at 3899 9773. Thank you.


